<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gregory of Nyssa</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 335 – c. 394</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century">4th century</a> · Also known as Gregory Nyssen</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gregory of Nyssa was an early Christian theologian, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of Nyssa from 372 to 376 and from 378 until his death, and with his elder brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basil-of-caesarea">Basil of Caesarea</a> and their friend <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gregory-of-nazianzus">Gregory of Nazianzus</a> one of the Cappadocian Fathers. Deposed by a synod in 376, he regained his see in 378 and took part in the First Council of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> in 381. He contributed to the doctrine of the Trinity and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicene-creed">Nicene Creed</a>, defining the Trinity as one essence in three persons, and his thought, influenced by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/origen">Origen</a>, has drawn new scholarly interest since the mid-twentieth century, particularly on universal salvation. He was the first person in recorded history to write against all forms of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gregory-of-nyssa.jpg" alt="Gregory of Nyssa" width="330" height="449" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:St._Gregory_of_Nyssa.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Gregory of Nyssa</b> wrote fifteen Homilies on the Song, comparing the bride to the soul and the groom to God, and Augustine, following Ambrose of Milan, read it as the wedding of Christ and the Church.</blockquote><a href="/songs/song-of-songs">Song of Songs</a></li></ul></section>
